WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.490 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A school excursion has a strict timetable
and a robot guide must turn

2
00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:08.390 line:1% position:50% size:90%
through precise angles. Can we solve both
challenges?

3
00:00:09.386 --> 00:00:14.116 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Today we are learning to solve multi-step
time and angle problems.

4
00:00:15.128 --> 00:00:19.678 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Our learning goal is to solve multi-step
time and angle problems.

5
00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:25.510 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Success criteria: choose a useful model,
calculate accurately and explain

6
00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:26.540 line:1% position:50% size:90%
each answer.

7
00:00:27.579 --> 00:00:32.539 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Read the departure and arrival times from
the same service on the timetable.

8
00:00:32.609 --> 00:00:35.549 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Different rows can refer to different
journeys.

9
00:00:36.557 --> 00:00:38.957 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A timeline makes the journey visible.

10
00:00:39.077 --> 00:00:43.957 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Begin at eight twenty-five, move to nine
o'clock and then move to nine ten.

11
00:00:44.983 --> 00:00:49.003 line:1% position:50% size:90%
How many minutes pass between eight
twenty-five and nine ten?

12
00:00:49.043 --> 00:00:51.413 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Estimate first, then calculate.

13
00:00:56.472 --> 00:01:01.232 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Thirty-five minutes to nine o'clock plus
ten minutes to nine ten makes forty-

14
00:01:01.232 --> 00:01:02.272 line:1% position:50% size:90%
five minutes.

15
00:01:03.279 --> 00:01:07.929 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Twenty-four-hour time removes confusion
between morning and afternoon.

16
00:01:07.979 --> 00:01:11.569 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Thirteen fifteen means one fifteen in the
afternoon.

17
00:01:12.598 --> 00:01:17.318 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A train departs at thirteen fifteen and
arrives at fourteen oh five.

18
00:01:17.348 --> 00:01:19.308 line:1% position:50% size:90%
How long is the journey?

19
00:01:24.301 --> 00:01:28.171 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Forty-five minutes to fourteen hundred,
then five more minutes.

20
00:01:28.211 --> 00:01:30.431 line:1% position:50% size:90%
The journey lasts fifty minutes.

21
00:01:31.479 --> 00:01:36.129 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Subtracting the minute numbers alone is a
mistake when you cross an hour.

22
00:01:36.159 --> 00:01:38.489 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Use the full timeline instead.

23
00:01:39.553 --> 00:01:43.993 line:1% position:50% size:90%
For a deadline, consider arrival time, not
just departure time.

24
00:01:44.113 --> 00:01:47.483 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Arriving after an event begins may be too
late.

25
00:01:48.479 --> 00:01:50.729 line:1% position:50% size:90%
You must arrive before ten.

26
00:01:50.759 --> 00:01:53.779 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A service arrives at nine thirty-five.

27
00:01:53.819 --> 00:01:55.619 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Does it meet the deadline?

28
00:02:00.627 --> 00:02:05.187 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Yes. Nine thirty-five is twenty-five
minutes before ten, so

29
00:02:05.187 --> 00:02:07.037 line:1% position:50% size:90%
the service meets the deadline.

30
00:02:08.059 --> 00:02:11.409 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Remember to include walking and waiting
time.

31
00:02:11.449 --> 00:02:15.339 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Reaching the station exactly when the bus
leaves is risky.

32
00:02:16.374 --> 00:02:20.444 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A bus departs at nine twenty and arrives
at ten oh five.

33
00:02:20.464 --> 00:02:22.644 line:1% position:50% size:90%
How many minutes does the trip take?

34
00:02:27.662 --> 00:02:31.102 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Forty minutes to ten o'clock, then five
more minutes.

35
00:02:31.152 --> 00:02:33.622 line:1% position:50% size:90%
The trip lasts forty-five minutes.

36
00:02:34.661 --> 00:02:36.991 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Check that your answer is sensible.

37
00:02:37.081 --> 00:02:40.921 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A journey crossing an hour can be longer
than the difference between the

38
00:02:40.921 --> 00:02:42.071 line:1% position:50% size:90%
minute digits.

39
00:02:43.079 --> 00:02:47.419 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A train leaves at eleven thirty-five and
arrives at twelve twenty.

40
00:02:47.419 --> 00:02:50.229 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Calculate the duration using noon.

41
00:02:55.288 --> 00:02:59.488 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Twenty-five minutes to noon plus twenty
minutes afterwards makes forty-five

42
00:02:59.488 --> 00:03:00.168 line:1% position:50% size:90%
minutes.

43
00:03:01.167 --> 00:03:05.737 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Read the correct timetable entry, draw a
timeline, cross the hour boundary

44
00:03:05.737 --> 00:03:07.197 line:1% position:50% size:90%
and add the intervals.

45
00:03:08.197 --> 00:03:10.437 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Think about a school bus timetable.

46
00:03:10.567 --> 00:03:14.777 line:1% position:50% size:90%
The departure time tells us when to be at
the stop, while the arrival time

47
00:03:14.777 --> 00:03:17.417 line:1% position:50% size:90%
tells us when we can reach our
destination.

48
00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:22.450 line:1% position:50% size:90%
If the bus is delayed, our original plan
may no longer work.

49
00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:26.620 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A good traveller checks whether there is
enough time before an important

50
00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:27.490 line:1% position:50% size:90%
appointment.

51
00:03:28.509 --> 00:03:30.509 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Try a different strategy.

52
00:03:30.529 --> 00:03:34.529 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Instead of counting every minute, split
the journey at the nearest whole

53
00:03:34.529 --> 00:03:36.749 line:1% position:50% size:90%
hour and add the two intervals.

54
00:03:37.757 --> 00:03:42.517 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A journey from nine thirty-five to ten
o'clock takes twenty-five minutes.

55
00:03:42.517 --> 00:03:46.777 line:1% position:50% size:90%
We know this because sixty minus
thirty-five equals twenty-five.

56
00:03:47.821 --> 00:03:50.401 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Check your answer by counting forward.

57
00:03:50.431 --> 00:03:54.851 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Start at nine thirty-five, add twenty-five
minutes, and you should reach ten

58
00:03:54.851 --> 00:03:55.631 line:1% position:50% size:90%
o'clock.

59
00:03:56.629 --> 00:04:00.909 line:1% position:50% size:90%
If you have to change buses, calculate the
waiting time between the first

60
00:04:00.909 --> 00:04:02.949 line:1% position:50% size:90%
arrival and the second departure.

61
00:04:02.969 --> 00:04:05.499 line:1% position:50% size:90%
That wait is part of the whole trip.

62
00:04:06.565 --> 00:04:10.775 line:1% position:50% size:90%
The most useful first step in a word
problem is deciding what is being

63
00:04:10.775 --> 00:04:15.785 line:1% position:50% size:90%
asked. Do you need a duration, a finishing
time or an angle size?

64
00:04:16.789 --> 00:04:21.539 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Imagine a workshop starting at nine
forty-five and lasting thirty minutes.

65
00:04:21.549 --> 00:04:26.029 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Fifteen minutes takes you to ten, and
another fifteen to ten fifteen.

66
00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:30.897 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A full rotation is three hundred and sixty
degrees.

67
00:04:30.907 --> 00:04:35.257 line:1% position:50% size:90%
If a robot turns through ninety degrees
four times, it returns to its

68
00:04:35.257 --> 00:04:36.497 line:1% position:50% size:90%
starting direction.

69
00:04:37.516 --> 00:04:41.776 line:1% position:50% size:90%
When two angles form a straight line,
their measures total one hundred and

70
00:04:41.776 --> 00:04:45.906 line:1% position:50% size:90%
eighty degrees. This fact lets us find a
missing angle.

71
00:04:46.949 --> 00:04:50.079 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Suppose one angle is seventy degrees.

72
00:04:50.079 --> 00:04:54.499 line:1% position:50% size:90%
The adjacent angle on a straight line is
one hundred and ten degrees,

73
00:04:54.499 --> 00:04:57.209 line:1% position:50% size:90%
because the sum is one hundred and eighty.

74
00:04:58.227 --> 00:05:02.717 line:1% position:50% size:90%
If an event finishes at eleven twenty and
lasts forty-five minutes, work

75
00:05:02.717 --> 00:05:06.887 line:1% position:50% size:90%
backwards: twenty minutes to eleven, then
twenty-five more to ten thirty-

76
00:05:06.887 --> 00:05:07.547 line:1% position:50% size:90%
five.

77
00:05:08.565 --> 00:05:11.885 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A useful check is to reverse the
calculation.

78
00:05:11.915 --> 00:05:16.715 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Add forty-five minutes to ten thirty-five
and confirm that you reach eleven

79
00:05:16.715 --> 00:05:17.335 line:1% position:50% size:90%
twenty.

80
00:05:18.341 --> 00:05:22.881 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Now invent a problem that combines a
travel duration with a turn.

81
00:05:22.921 --> 00:05:27.541 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Explain which part needs a timeline and
which needs an angle diagram.

82
00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:30.790 line:1% position:50% size:90%
Estimate before you calculate.

83
00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:35.180 line:1% position:50% size:90%
A journey from eleven thirty-five to
twelve twenty must be shorter than an

84
00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:38.600 line:1% position:50% size:90%
hour, because twelve thirty-five would be
one hour later.

85
00:05:39.633 --> 00:05:43.553 line:1% position:50% size:90%
You can explain your thinking with words,
numbers or a timeline.

86
00:05:43.583 --> 00:05:47.173 line:1% position:50% size:90%
The strongest answer shows where each
interval comes from.
